claimed: json-cst-batch-road -- the lossless CST road, measured and fixed.

Operator question after json-parse-fast-road: why is the lossless road
slow, and how is it made fast.

Measured, 1.68 MB:  Json.lossless 244 ms against the fast road's 17.
The breakdown says it is not JSON and not the tree -- it is that
Json.cst feeds the source ONE CHARACTER AT A TIME through the effect
system (Writer.tell + flatMap per char), then two transducer stages,
then a LazyList. Just moving the characters costs 61-71 ms, four times
the entire fast parse; the real work is ~28 ms each for lexing,
instructions and building.

The fix is not new machinery: `Parse.full(sc, step)` is documented as
"the common case: a per-token driver with no state of its own", which
is exactly JsonParse.instrs ("no cross-token state"). Xml already uses
fullWith and Yaml a hand loop -- JSON was the ONLY codec still on the
per-char pipeline, and the batch road it should have taken has existed
all along.

Verified on one input already: the tree is IDENTICAL (==), render is
still byte-identical, 244 -> 111 ms. Before landing, the equality gets
the corpus and the PREFIX SWEEP that TestJsonValue already has for the
value roads, because a lossless tree is a stronger claim than a value.

The streaming pipeline keeps its reason to exist: incremental reparse
and input that is not a String in hand.

Files: okay-codec Json.scala, tests, specs/codecs.md, boards.
